turns.text:
The connection drop happens during one of the queries in the try block. The error is caught, then the catch block tries to `ROLLBACK` on the dead connection.

If `ROLLBACK` itself fails (which it likely does on a dead connection), that failure error is thrown instead of the original connection error. The original error is lost—the caller only sees the ROLLBACK error.

The database automatically rolls back the uncommitted transaction when the connection dies, so the write never happens. The `finally` block still safely releases the client.

The route handler gets an error (though not the clearest one), responds with 500, and the request is rejected. The data wasn't modified, which is correct—but the error message doesn't say "connection dropped," it says "ROLLBACK failed" or similar.

If you wanted better error reporting, suppress the ROLLBACK error and throw the original instead:

```javascript
} catch (err) {
  try {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  } catch (rollbackErr) {
    // ignore ROLLBACK failure, original error is more important
  }
  throw err;
}
```

