turns.text:
If the connection drops mid-transaction, the catch block attempts ROLLBACK on a dead connection, which throws an error that replaces the original error. The caller never knows what actually happened. The database will eventually roll back the transaction on its end (automatic on disconnect), but the client can't acknowledge that.

More critically: if the connection drops during COMMIT, the mutation may already be committed on the server while the client gets an error. The caller can't distinguish between "safely rolled back" and "partially committed."

The fix is to catch ROLLBACK failures separately and preserve the original error:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // Connection is dead; transaction rolled back server-side
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This preserves the original error while gracefully handling ROLLBACK failures. The caller still can't know if COMMIT succeeded server-side, but at least the diagnostic error is intact.

