turns.text:
The connection drop during the write query throws an error. The catch block then tries to ROLLBACK on the dead connection, which also fails. That second error overwrites the first, so the route handler gets an unhelpful error message instead of the actual connection failure. The broken client releases back to the pool, where it can cause problems for the next request that gets it.

You need to guard ROLLBACK and treat connection errors specially:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");
    const res = await client.query(text, params);
    await client.query("COMMIT");
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    try {
      await client.query("ROLLBACK");
    } catch (rollbackErr) {
      // Connection is broken; don't mask the original error
    }
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

This way the original error surfaces, and the broken connection is still released (the pool handles cleanup on reconnect).

