turns.text:
Looking at the write() function:

```javascript
async function write(text, params) {
  const client = await pool.connect();      // 1. Get connection
  try {
    await client.query("BEGIN");             // 2. Start transaction
    const res = await client.query(text, params);  // 3. Run the write
    await client.query("COMMIT");            // 4. Commit
    return res.rows[0];
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");          // 5. On error, rollback
    throw err;
  } finally {
    client.release();                        // 6. Always release
  }
}
```

If the connection drops during any query (2–4), that await throws. The catch block then tries to execute ROLLBACK on the same dead connection, which throws again. That second error overwrites the first, so the caller sees a confusing ROLLBACK failure instead of the actual problem that caused it.

The server-side transaction is already aborted when the connection dies, so the ROLLBACK is redundant—but the code doesn't account for that. The real issue is error masking: the original write failure gets hidden.

The finally block releases the client back to the pool regardless, which is correct, but by then you've lost visibility into what went wrong. A better pattern wraps the ROLLBACK itself in error handling:

```javascript
catch (err) {
  try {
    await client.query("ROLLBACK");
  } catch (rollbackErr) {
    // Connection already dead; original error matters more
  }
  throw err;  // Throw the original error
}
```

This preserves the real failure and doesn't let cleanup mistakes hide it.

