No — raising the pool size would just delay the symptom. The real bug is in `db.js:11-16`: `withClient` calls `client.release()` only after `fn(client)` succeeds, with no `try/finally`. If `fn` throws, the client is checked out forever. `pool.log` shows `idle=0` at every sample while `waiting` climbs steadily (7→11→14→19→23→28) — connections are leaking, not just busy. Fix:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

